# PowerShell scripts must reach Windows with CRLF endings. Left to itself git
# would check them out with whatever the platform default is, and a .ps1 with
# LF endings is a second way to get parse errors that point at the wrong lines.
#
# The files themselves are deliberately plain ASCII with a UTF-8 BOM: Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 reads a .ps1 as ANSI without one, so a single em dash is enough
# to break the parse. Keep them ASCII.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must keep LF, including when checked out on Windows — CRLF in a
# shebang line makes the interpreter unfindable.
*.sh text eol=lf
